{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1548016-left-handed-legacy-the-wardens-of-omelas-tw-discussion-of-harm-to-children","id":1548016,"title":"Left-Handed Legacy: The Wardens of Omelas (TW: discussion of harm to children)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-01-28T23:34:37-08:00","id":1548016,"title":"Left-Handed Legacy: The Wardens of Omelas (TW: discussion of harm to children)","content":"&quot;The Wardens of Omelas&quot; is the modern name for a Legacy, or a group of legacies, which have existed since Mages have lived side-by-side with mortal communities. Sleeper societies have long believed that the sacrifice of the young and innocent could bring prosperity to the community. Unfortunately, Mages know that there is truth to this belief.<br /> <br /> The Attainments of this Legacy require the confinement, torture, and eventual death of an innocent child. The suffering and death form a special Sacrament Yantra which fuels the Attainments themselves, Fate-based abilities that grant increasing levels of fortune and prosperity to an increasingly-large community of people. The more remaining lifespan the sacrifice had, and the higher their Integrity (or equivalent), the more effective the sacrifice.<br /> <br /> Is this something, do you think?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1168-hobbitguy1420","name":"HobbitGuy1420","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"332"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-12T05:31:38-08:00","id":1548430,"title":"","content":"This is super evil, and has the potential to make a very memorable antagonist, but it definitely needs more than two paragraphs of development. What kind of powers were you thinking they'd have, and how mechanically would these sacrifices benefit the Warden(s) or their community at large? Would the child's caretakers still remember their their child is missing, or does the mage steal their memories as well? Do they leave a fetch in the child's place \u00e0 la changelings, perhaps to sicken and die in the victim's place so that the parents don't come snooping around? Do they have any special kind of empathy with youngsters, like the Dauphines of Wayward Children from Changeling 2e (who would probably be this Legacy's most ancient and implacable foes)? Most evilly of all, <i>do they ever use children as human shields</i>?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/856697-motor-city-breakdown-a-playable-changeling-setting-for-detroit-michigan\" target=\"_blank\">Motor City Breakdown</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/19D2lK7otRprpHsJz0aZF0WucpMtoEgGQ2ExZJ-ud0a4/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">Devil's Night in the D</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1uFgCI8m4gJ5kg1DneG0Cgu18bmUpe4RxDNDfI824G6M/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">The Magical History Tour</a><br /> Accuracy Consultant on <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/AshesOfTheMotorCity.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Ashes of the Motor City</a><br /> Editor, Compiler, and Senior Contributor to <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1OxlnZCafIZtgPMCfikl0DJvWAwZKJDLk6lxh9hFPB1g/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Tenebrous Seas</a><br /> <b>Current Project(s)</b>: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/15v9c9IWq_HOjZoYPWqceTxQ0-z1epdN4pt5F6nMUYpE/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">We Have Fought the Long Defeat</a>, a Late Antiquity/Early Medieval Dark Era for <i>Genius: The Transgression</i>; <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/12IQd13VQ0kHFY19yUBBkqVA-cKT0gZlByOkgYRkb-Ss/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Just Before the Dawn</i></a><i>,</i> a 1970s PtH Dark Era","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6327-super_dave","name":"Super_Dave","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-12T08:59:38-08:00","id":1548436,"title":"","content":"My thought is that they'd grant Fate blessings to the entire community; mostly narrative effects, but the community they're in suffers less bad luck and more good luck, as long as the Warden is maintaining the proper sacrifices. Businesses move into the area, wages stay up, car accidents go down. The only type of misfortune that <i>increases</i> rather than <i>decreases</i> when a Warden is active in a community are missing persons cases. The Warden herself experiences the same benefits, but nothing particular beyond that. Part of the point of the concept is the conflict between their explicitly benign goals and their explicitly malign methods. They do help their communities a great deal, and they convince themselves they're the good guys, but they do it by <i>torturing children.</i> The archetypal Warden, accused of using children as a human shield, would be offended and appalled. They don't do this for themselves, they do this for their community. They're not <i>bad people.</i><br /> <br /> I don't think the Wardens as a group have special powers to cause people to forget their charges or replace them. They <i>do</i> learn to read and manipulate children in order to lure them properly.Those with the ability to magically cover their tracks absolutely do so - Life for simulacra, Mind to make people disregard their losses, Death to make the bodies look like they died a different way. Others avoid suspicion in more mundane ways.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1168-hobbitguy1420","name":"HobbitGuy1420","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"332"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-12T12:02:35-08:00","id":1548444,"title":"","content":"They sound a lot like Seers (maybe even a ministry or methodology?). Their favourite oblation is human sacrifice, whilst the Exarchs have enough power over the setting that they can do basically anything you want narratively (no need to deal with the limits of attainments).<br /> <br /> (<i>Reign of the Exarchs</i> has a Seer pylon who are engaged in their own mini-utopia project, an idealised English village. They don't murder children for it but it definitely has that vibe.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T10:16:18-08:00","id":1548473,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure about these guys as Seers. They're far more... <i>charitable</i> than Seers tend to be. These guys are closer to the idea of &quot;hideous deeds toward kind ends&quot; than &quot;keep the masses ground beneath the heel.&quot; Honestly, I could see these guys popping up in the Silver Ladder, as a twisted reflection of their paternalistic attitude toward Sleepers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1168-hobbitguy1420","name":"HobbitGuy1420","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"332"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T10:34:29-08:00","id":1548475,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HobbitGuy1420</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1548016-left-handed-legacy-the-wardens-of-omelas-tw-discussion-of-harm-to-children?p=1548473#post1548473\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They're far more... <i>charitable</i> than Seers tend to be.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're child murderers in return for local prosperity. It's not exactly a subtle moral dilemma. Their 'kind ends' are clearly nonsense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T11:09:11-08:00","id":1548476,"title":"","content":"Yes, but it's <i>self-deceptive</i> nonsense. The Wardens are villains who have convinced themselves that they're heroes. It doesn't hurt that the benefits they offer the community are significant, if ill-defined. We're talking &quot;Healthy, prosperous neighborhood full of happy families with drinkable water in the middle of Flint, MI&quot; levels of fortune.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1168-hobbitguy1420","name":"HobbitGuy1420","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"332"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T12:57:52-08:00","id":1548478,"title":"","content":"I want to inquire more deeply into the idea of a pre-existing foundation to the idea of such sacrifice, because on the face of it it's hard to see mages organically arriving at a progression towards a level of blessing bestowal that is apparently disproportionate with Arcanum levels that originates in horrific child murder. How can a person who wants the one really countenance the other? Sure, there's supposed to be self-deception involved in it, but that's taking a <i>lot</i> of extra steps to get there, not just morally but mystically.<br /> <br /> I think it also sets them apart from a lot of other Left-Handed Legacies in a way that feels overpowered without driving purpose in comparison. It's like, how and why should this particular thing yield such a higher return than the practices of any given group, up to and including the Reapers of souls?<br /> <br /> I feel as though if a person wants to invoke Ursula LeGuin's allegorical story like that, it would be better served as a locally confined Mystery. There could conceivably be a Legacy built around the Mystery, a degree to which they can key their Attainments off of it, but still something where the greater bulk of the effect and narrative sought is external to the mages and it boxes them in a bit (albeit possibly with a plot hook of them investigating the possibility of extending it or finding ways to make the same Mystery appear elsewhere).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T14:19:40-08:00","id":1548481,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HobbitGuy1420</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1548016-left-handed-legacy-the-wardens-of-omelas-tw-discussion-of-harm-to-children?p=1548476#post1548476\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, but it's <i>self-deceptive</i> nonsense. The Wardens are villains who have convinced themselves that they're heroes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but why should players care? Presumably most villains think they're heroes in some sense, but that usually doesn't stop them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We're talking &quot;Healthy, prosperous neighborhood full of happy families with drinkable water in the middle of Flint, MI&quot; levels of fortune. </div> </div> </div>I think that's moving in the right direction. Like, making it a trolley problem at least sets up that there will be real consequences to ending the mages' practice.<br /> <br /> Come to think of it, your description brings to mind the Harper Family from <i>Intruders</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T14:23:45-08:00","id":1548482,"title":"","content":"The intent was always to make this a Trolley Problem situation. The Warden is keeping alive a community that <i>should</i> be dead - not just alive, but prosperous, happy, healthy, peaceful, etc. At the cost of the slow deaths of children. Kill the warden and watch the community die, or leave the warden alive and know that the health of that small town is at the cost of innocents.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude1e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1168-hobbitguy1420","name":"HobbitGuy1420","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"332"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-13T15:29:02-08:00","id":1548485,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1548016-left-handed-legacy-the-wardens-of-omelas-tw-discussion-of-harm-to-children?p=1548478#post1548478\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I want to inquire more deeply into the idea of a pre-existing foundation to the idea of such sacrifice, because on the face of it it's hard to see mages organically arriving at a progression towards a level of blessing bestowal that is apparently disproportionate with Arcanum levels that originates in horrific child murder. How can a person who wants the one really countenance the other? Sure, there's supposed to be self-deception involved in it, but that's taking a <i>lot</i> of extra steps to get there, not just morally but mystically.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, on a Doylist basis, I've seen the idea of &quot;Sacrifice a child for the good of the community&quot; in several places - not just the LeGuin story, but <i>American Gods</i> and a couple other stories, too. I'm not 100% sure if there's historical folklore, belief, or practice behind the idea, but I felt it was worth the idea of inclusion in the game because of the inherent moral quandry it presents: the health of an entire community, vs. the suffering of those sacrificed toward that end. <br /> <br /> On the Watsonian end, I haven't entirely worked out all the details for how and why these sacrifices work. If this <i>was</i> just a Mystery, I don't think it's geographically-limited. It feels appropriate for it to be more widely-available than that, because it's just that much more horrible if that dreadful choice is availablein<i> any </i>dying community.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1168-hobbitguy1420","name":"HobbitGuy1420","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"332"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-18T15:25:25-08:00","id":1548572,"title":"","content":"I'll be honest, if I was faced with this I would regard the moral dilemma as crude. There's not much nuance to be derived from the image of murdered children in comparison to other, more indirect, morally dubious foundations for community prosperity. And even taken on its own terms, I would see it as a compromise that taints the value of whatever it creates back in return.<br /> <br /> As far as the idea that this is a widespread thing... is it a scenario that players would be intending to encounter frequently? If they find it reprehensible, do they have an option to be on a globe-trotting adventure dealing with it (and I suppose running repeatedly into communities that go to shit once the atrocity providing their golden ticket is ended)? I find other Left-Handed Legacies at least provide a template that can be more readily slotted into a variety of scenarios while also contributing to a sense of the larger setting (and even then, in practice, a lot of them are probably still not very large).<br /> <br /> Sure the idea is horrible, but to what end?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-18T16:28:46-08:00","id":1548574,"title":"","content":"I would say that some Moloch imagery would be pretty obvious, but I feel like that would be a bit too overtly malevolent to really fit in with the theme that you are going for. Still, the historical practice of human sacrifice during times of hardship, as well as claims of it as a polemical tool, still make me wonder if you could work them in there in some capacity, perhaps as with Moloch reverence being featured in the Yantras of some particularly cold-blooded strains of the Legacy. <br /> <br /> In any case, if you do want them to earnestly believe in the morality of their actions, there are some theological and magical avenues that they could utilize to alleviate their guilt. They could see the sacrifices as a sort of mercy, as while it may not free souls from the phenomenal world entirely, it could still liberate them from incarnate existence, which they could see as a form of debasement. Of course, the problem with that route is that they would really have no reason to want worldly benefits for the sacrifice at all, but I think that they could make rationalizations for that. Perhaps they try to defend communities of Awakened and Sleepwalkers, as well as individuals who they think have a better chance of Awakening. Thus, the children would be sacrificed so that others might live long enough to come to Awakening, which could be seen as an objective good. Also, you are dealing with mages. If the Legacy focuses heavily on the soul, then they would not really have a reason to hold children in any special regard, and they could thus say that they are &quot;just&quot; performing human sacrifices. <br /> <br /> I do agree that it would be difficult to portray the Legacy in a well-meaning light, but even if the Legacy ultimately lacks in serious ethical drama, I think that you can still incorporate them in your game in some capacity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}